2. G.E.R.A.L.D. → NEXERATH: recovered-history pipeline
Recovery stream	Candidate reuse	Processing and verification	Display value
Bottle / window glass	Cast accents, polished light paths, enclosure panels when suitably characterized	Sort by glass family; remove contaminants; controlled remelting and annealing, or mechanically reuse known panes	Original city and site visible in cast layers
Copper wire and motors	Coils, bus conductors, sculpted conductors	Strip, measure insulation and continuity; rewind; isolate exposed power nodes	Visible copper spirals
Speaker / motor magnets	Induction rotors, shuttles, coupling links	Verify grade qualitatively, magnetization and corrosion protection; secure mechanically	Moving field sources
Bearings and shafts	Turbines, displacers, pendulum pivots	Inspect runout, pitting, friction and lubrication compatibility	Motion from retired machines
Aluminum / steel / brass	Frame, fins, turbine vanes, brackets, heat sinks	Identify alloy where necessary; inspect cracks; re-machine, coat or heat treat	Patina and engraved origin
Springs and flexures	Tuned vibration harvesters	Inspect fatigue damage; characterize stiffness and resonant frequency	Kinetic reeds
Retired circuit boards	Displayed history fragments or characterized control parts	Remove batteries and contaminated components; encapsulate or isolate ornamental boards	1992-era circuitry as a visible time capsule
Historic relays / gears	Operational mechanisms if electrically and mechanically suitable, otherwise protected display	Document age and provenance; test; avoid unverified mains-voltage reuse	A 1942 component with its own story
Crucial manufacturing distinction: reclaimed glass makes extraordinary sculpture, optical pathways, and protective shells. Pressure-bearing or chemically critical vessels require validated material and manufacturing processes; make a properly engineered inner chamber if random cullet or unknown reclaimed glass cannot be qualified for that duty. That is a fabrication requirement, not an abandonment of reclaimed-glass artistry.
Provenance passport (one per installed item)
`node_id`, `component_id`, `source_site`, `recovery_date`.
`estimated_year_or_era` and evidence/confidence—a part thought to be from 1942 is not labeled as historical fact without provenance.
`material`, `original_function`, `new_function`, `inspection_results`.
`rework_steps`, `replaced_parts`, `known_hazards`, `maker`, `photograph`.
`maintenance_history`, `measured_contribution` where instrumented.
Each public node has a metal plate and a local/QR-accessible parts ledger. The installation is a museum with an electrical output.
