3. Family architecture: one lattice, several deployment classes
Class	Where it lives	Primary energy inputs	Meaningful job	Distinct engineering needs
NEXERATH-A	General sculptural core	Thermal, flex, light, airflow	Prove source aggregation	Standard module mounts and bus
NEXERATH-S	Interior sanctuary	Temperature transients, low vibration, light	Local sensing, timed illumination	Whisper-quiet, touch-safe, aesthetic priority
NEXERATH-W	Pond / fountain	Ripple, water level cycling, solar, wind	Water/environment logging	Fouling, float seals, corrosion
NEXERATH-C	Civic plaza	Sun, convection, wind, structural vibration	Site telemetry and public readout	Wind loads, vandal resistance, public safety
NEXERATH-R	G.E.R.A.L.D. restoration outpost	Site-specific solar, thermal, vibration, runoff	Environmental sentinel / beacon	Autonomy, weatherization, maintenance interval
Counting rule for THE 77: these begin as branches of the NEXERATH family. A branch receives its own numbered invention entry only when it introduces substantially different functional engineering, validation, and a separate usable design package. Aesthetic changes and simply swapping salvaged parts do not inflate the count.
---
4. Reference mechanical architecture
A vertical sculpture is the canonical first full-form installation: transparent glass and copper around a protected load-bearing frame, with modules plugged into standardized mechanical/electrical sockets.
Standardized interfaces (proposed, to be refined)
Module footprint: 100 × 150 mm mounting land; slotted pattern to accept nonuniform reclaimed parts.
Power connector: keyed, shrouded, two-pole low-voltage connector per source; never join raw outputs directly.
Data connector: optional isolated low-voltage sensor link, separate from energy transport.
Serviceability: remove one module without dismantling the frame or disturbing other sources.
Visibility: at least one working conversion mechanism visible per installed class.
Access: protected high-voltage or moving machinery inaccessible to visitors; source wiring and buffers inside a service-only zone.
Physical form: a hand-built base of reclaimed steel/brass supports a transparent vertical spine, curved glass vanes, exposed but guarded copper windings, a deliberately visible storage indicator, and a small provenance plate. The form is unique; the mounting and electrical behavior are standardized.
---
5. Harvest modules — preserve the ambition, specify the machinery
H1 — Thermal Breath / Pulse Chamber
Purpose: make heat exchange visible as pressure, volume, or displacement changes; extract a share of mechanically useful movement.
Two operating modes:
Transient mode: warming a chamber moves a diaphragm or magnetic piston; cooling restores it. Produces charge events during changes, not continuous power from a static uniform temperature.
Cyclic mode: add an engineered temperature-cycle mechanism (for example a low-temperature Stirling-like displacer, heat-switching linkage, or deliberately timed thermal contacts) between a hot side and a cooler sink. Aim for repeatable oscillation under a sustained gradient.
Harvesting path: diaphragm/piston → guided magnet shuttle → wound copper coil → rectification/conditioning → source buffer. Magnetic coupling may keep a working-fluid chamber wholly sealed.
Fabrication needs: pressure-appropriate vessel or lined transparent chamber, welded/brazed heat-transfer surfaces if appropriate, leak-test jig, low-friction guide, adjustable return spring, coil winder, force/displacement fixture.
Working fluid workstream: first prove displacement with air or another benign test medium. Evaluate specialized low-boiling fluids only with chemical compatibility, containment, pressure rating, recovery/disposal and operating-temperature analysis. The common glass hand-boiler visual can guide aesthetics but does not prove an extractable continuous power cycle by itself.
Observables: warm/cold face temperatures, pressure or displacement, stroke frequency, open-circuit voltage, loaded harvested joules and leakage rate.
H2 — Convection Organ / Turbine
Purpose: harvest pressure and flow generated by temperature differences or ambient draft through a sculpted vertical passage.
Path: transparent chimney → designed inlet throat / hot zone / outlet → low-friction rotor → magnet-stator microgenerator → source buffer.
Development knobs: chimney height, cross-section, solar absorptivity, buoyancy, side-wind influence, turbine blade pitch, magnetic drag, bearing friction and electrical load. Build a removable rotor cassette so these can be changed without destroying the artwork.
Measurement: inlet/outlet temperature, airspeed and differential pressure where practical, unloaded RPM, loaded RPM and net energy delivered to buffer.
H3 — Vibration Garden / Flex Reeds
Purpose: capture actual available structural, acoustic or wind-induced mechanical vibration.
Path: tuned reclaimed spring-steel reed → small moving magnet with fixed coil or characterized piezo element → suitable low-loss harvester IC/rectifier → buffer.
Development knobs: mass, spring constant, damping, resonant frequency, base excitation, end stops and fatigue life. Use an adjustable-mass tuning jig and compare resonance peaks instead of guessing.
Measurement: excitation spectrum, frequency, displacement, peak voltage, captured joules, cyclic fatigue.
H4 — Magnetic Pendulum / Motion Cell
Purpose: common electromechanical interface for other sources (thermal stroke, deliberate touch, wind vane, rotating wheel, floating buoy).
Path: permanent-magnet motion relative to coil → rectifier → buffer. The return stroke may be harvested too; the external forcing and loss budget are always recorded.
Development knobs: coil turns, wire gauge, magnet spacing, gap, shuttle friction, eddy-current damping, duty cycle and magnetic detent effects.
H5 — Ripple Harp / Float Generator
Purpose: harvest actual surface-wave and water-level motion in NEXERATH-W.
Path: sealed float follows water → linkage or magnetic coupling across a static seal → shuttle/rotor generator → buffer.
Design work: biofouling, debris screens, flood excursion limits, safe fish/wildlife spacing, corrosion and recoverable maintenance. Water-facing hardware must detach cleanly.
H6 — Radiant Differential / Sun-Shade Plate
Purpose: collect useful electrical energy from temperature gradients and sunlight while visually demonstrating thermal transfer.
Paths: (a) thermoelectric device clamped between characterized hot and cold surfaces; (b) calibrated PV module; (c) bimetal or shape-memory actuator driving a slow mechanical event. These are separate mechanisms that can share mounting but should retain independent metering.
Design work: heat-sink geometry, thermal contacts, source impedance, actual hot/cold temperatures, actuator cycle life. No claims about TEG power without measured conditions.
---
6. The Harvest Lattice — common electrical architecture
Do not parallel unlike raw harvesters. Every energy source gets its own electrically characterized front end before the common storage system.
```text
Thermal magnetic coil -- AC rectifier / harvester -- local buffer --\
Convection generator -- rectifier / load match ------- local buffer ---\
Vibration reed ------ piezo/induction harvester ----- local buffer ----> isolated / protected energy OR-ing
Ripple generator ---- rectifier / converter -------- local buffer ---/                |
TEG / PV ------------ matched DC harvest converter - local buffer --/                 v
                                                                       COMMON SUPERCAP BUFFER
                                                                                |
                                                                      regulated charging logic
                                                                                |
                                                               protected battery (if justified)
                                                                                |
                                                                   switched useful loads
                                                                                |
                                                            telemetry / display / beacon / LED
```
Input-channel design specification
Each source channel documents:
input type (AC / DC / high-impedance pulses), min/max voltage and typical source impedance;
conditioning method (rectifier, synchronous circuit, impedance match or MPPT where appropriate);
cold-start behavior and leakage when idle;
reverse-current prevention so a dormant source cannot drain the others;
capacitor voltage/current measurement points;
source ID and calibration information.
Suggested rail strategy
Experimental source domain: floating or isolated low-energy source, no fixed universal raw voltage.
Shared capture domain: supercapacitor buffer with rated cell-balancing and overvoltage protection as needed.
Output: regulated low-voltage DC rail to intermittently operate the chosen job; 3.3 V electronics is a useful initial target.
Optional autonomy extension: protected LiFePO4 storage with compatible charge controller, low-temperature charge protection and accessible disconnect. It must not silently become the source of supposed harvested output.
Important bookkeeping: storage and regulation move energy in time; they are not new energy sources. Each module's contribution is measured before and after its conversion electronics. Decorative LEDs consume part of the harvested energy, so there is always a separate tally for visual output and the useful service.
Energy accounting
For a capacitor:
`E_joules = 0.5 × C_farads × (V_final² − V_initial²)`
Example: a 10 F test capacitor increasing from 2.0 V to 3.0 V has gained 25 J = 0.00694 Wh, before accounting for losses and measurement error. That small increment is still a successful measurable harvest event if it comes from the identified external input.
For a useful load:
`E_load = integral(P_load × dt)`; approximately `P × hours` for steady loads.
Performance dashboard, displayed per module and total:
`Input source / measured gradient or motion`
`Gross transducer output (J or Wh)`
`Net added to shared storage (J or Wh)`
`Overhead: controller + converters + sensing + LEDs (J or Wh)`
`Energy delivered to useful service (J or Wh)`
`Energy lost in conversion / idle leakage`
`Runtime supported at selected duty cycle`
An honest display makes a milliwatt as worth observing as a watt; it also makes future optimization experimentally meaningful.
