8. Prototype BOM — a procurement and salvage map
Planning allowances only, USD, not quotes; salvage can reduce purchased cost while increasing inspection and fabrication labor. All mission-critical power-storage and pressure components are selected for verified suitability, even if new components are required in the first build.
Subassembly	Quantity / basis	Preferred reclaimed contribution	Estimated purchased allowance
Frame, brackets, fasteners, feet	1 set	steel/aluminum/brass stock	$15–50
Transparent sculpture and guarded panels	1 set	verified panes, remelted cullet decorative sections	$20–90
Magnet/coil demonstrator	1–3 cartridges	speaker magnets, motor copper, shafts	$15–60
Thermal motion chamber and metering	1	copper fittings, fins, outer glass	$35–110
Convection chimney / generator	1	glass, vanes, bearings, motor parts	$20–80
Tunable vibration reed	1	spring steel, washers, piezo if appropriate	$10–40
Harvester conditioning front ends	3	housings, connectors, selected reclaimed electronics	$35–120
Supercap / regulated power	1 instrumented bank	enclosure and conductors	$25–80
Measurement and controller	1 board + sensors	salvaged display where suitable	$25–100
LEDs, engraving, provenance plate	1 set	glass lenses, salvaged lettering	$10–50
Misc. isolation, seals, test supplies	contingency	stock hardware	$25–75
Estimated purchase envelope	Bench / display-scale demonstrator	Recovered history actively used	$235–855
Excluded: existing machine-shop tools, specialty glass/pressure certifications, shop labor, site-specific outdoor/public structural design, laboratory measurement equipment if not already owned, and major tooling purchases.
Recovery-first procurement procedure
Inventory usable landfill-derived parts from G.E.R.A.L.D.
Identify missing functional specifications, not missing catalog SKUs.
Decide for each gap: machine / cast / wind / anneal / print / buy / collaborate.
Choose the option that achieves verified performance while preserving the historic-art character.
Record the part's origin and its test results.
---
9. Experimental gates — evidence for every ambitious function
Gate	Demonstration	Record / advancement criterion
G0 — Authentic source	Identify a real external input	Temperature differential, vibration input, airflow, surface-wave motion, or photon input logged
G1 — Transduction	Motion/light/heat gradient becomes electrical output	Repeatable waveform under load; baseline/control comparison
G2 — Storage	Source charges buffer	Before/after capacitor energy gain measured and repeatable
G3 — Multi-source aggregation	Three independently metered channels charge shared store	No backfeed; each source's incremental contribution identifiable
G4 — Useful work	Stored harvested energy performs a repeatable task	A real sensor reading, timestamp/log entry, indicator sequence, or similar duty-cycle service completed
G5 — Material lineage	Salvaged components verified and documented	Traceable passport for every conspicuous historical component
G6 — Mechanical life	Motion withstands repeated cycling	Defined test cycles, inspection interval, failure logs, replaceable wear parts
G7 — Gallery/public deployment	People can safely inspect a working machine	Stable structure, protected moving/energized parts, maintained enclosure, site review
Controls worth doing early
Compare warmed versus unheated thermal module; distinguish transient one-shot displacement from steady cycle.
Compare moving versus immobilized reed under the same measurement conditions.
Compare open generator, resistive load and energy-buffer load to identify electrical loading effects.
Compare each bus input alone, then combined, to reveal leakage or interference.
Measure controller overhead separately. For microharvesting, idle draw can determine whether the installation accumulates energy.
Targeted design intent: aim first for a repeatable positive net charge event and one genuinely self-supported low-power task; numerical output goals will be set from observed source conditions and module behavior, not invented as if already tested.
---
10. Safety and durability by design
NEXERATH invites close viewing, so safety belongs in the sculpture's engineering, not hidden in fine print.
Unknown reclaimed glass: segregate and characterize before heat treatment; properly anneal and inspect decorative casting. Use rated inner vessels for pressurized fluids and a guarded shell for public displays.
Low-boiling working fluids: prototype containment and motion first; choose any working fluid through pressure/temperature/flammability/toxicity/material compatibility review. Avoid casual use of toxic solvents or fragile makeshift pressure glass.
Recovered electronics: test insulation and contamination; retire unverified mains components to safe display roles; remove old cells/capacitors where appropriate.
Magnets and rotors: contain moving assemblies, retain magnets mechanically, prevent pinch and thrown-part hazards.
Electrical storage: limit voltage, fused/protected storage, safe service disconnects; handle lithium storage with certified protection and thermal safeguards.
Public installations: structural, wind, electrical, accessibility and environmental review specific to the site; anti-tamper panels without hiding core visual mechanisms.
Water modules: keep water and electricity separated through verified seals, drip paths and removable service cartridges.
Wildlife and ecosystem: no exposed pinch zones near animal access, no uncontrolled leaching from scrap-derived materials.
A unique salvage object should be more inspectable, not less, because its provenance and material properties are known and recorded.
---
11. Data, art, and learning interface
Instrument panel for visitors
`HEAT ΔT` · `AIRFLOW` · `VIBRATION` · `WATER MOTION` (only where installed).
Individual source LEDs that indicate real measured charge events, not decorative simulated production.
`ENERGY HARVESTED TODAY` and `ENERGY USED TODAY` with clear units.
One line identifying the useful function the energy is powering.
Source history rotating on a low-power panel or viewable through a QR-accessible archive.
Public story example (illustrative)
NEXERATH / NODE 007 — RECOVERED TIME  
Glass: reclaimed local bottle and window stream  
Copper: retired motor windings  
Historic feature: relay, estimated 1940s — pending provenance confirmation  
Circuit relic: motherboard, estimated early 1990s — for display, not active control  
Harvest classes: thermal / convection / vibration  
Job: periodic microclimate measurements and local display  
Builder's note: A landfill yielded the glass. A restoration system recovered the metal. A maker taught the pieces to work together.
This is not merely a QR code on a sculpture. It is an evolving material biography.
---
12. What makes the design stronger over time
Workstream A — Better mechanical-to-electrical capture
Develop swappable magnet/coil cartridge geometries.
Compare magnetic circuit efficiency, friction and electrical damping.
Add mechanical energy storage (spring/flywheel) only where a genuine external source charges it and it improves conversion efficiency or timing.
Workstream B — Better heat engines
Develop controlled thermal cycling, high-conductance warm/cold interfaces, displacement amplification and low-friction seals.
Characterize a closed fluid circuit only once containment and heat rejection are engineered.
Compare gas piston, fluid displacer, Stirling-like and bimetal approaches experimentally; artwork can accommodate more than one.
Workstream C — Better aggregation electronics
Optimize cold-start thresholds and idle leakage.
Match each transducer's electrical load rather than force all sources through one generic rectifier.
Duty-cycle sensing, communication and decorative lighting to the energy actually collected.
Workstream D — Better recovered materials
Characterize cullet blends and annealing schedules with a glass partner.
Develop modular glass mounts and replaceable seals; keep patina without compromising electrical contacts or structural integrity.
Build a local salvage-property database so later sculptures become easier and cheaper to engineer.
Workstream E — More useful jobs
Indoor climate sensor / timed data logger.
Restoration outpost environmental beacon.
Public demonstration of actual energy conversion and material histories.
Water-quality logger on a fountain/ripple version.
Networked low-power nodes where the measured energy budget supports communications.
---
13. What still needs to be learned (the productive backlog)
Which sites present sufficient recurring thermal or mechanical gradients to warrant a continuous-motion sculpture?
What combination of displacement, coil geometry and switching gives the best low-temperature thermal-module yield?
What is the minimum-startup turbine geometry for the available chimney height, warm-air flux and electrical load?
What is the aggregate idle-power floor, and how can it be reduced until every source earns its place?
Can we make a user-repairable rated thermal cartridge with reclaimed glass as its visible outer body?
Which recovered alloys and bearings achieve the target fatigue and corrosion performance?
How should energy output, environmental benefit and educational engagement be reported without conflating them?
What material genealogy can G.E.R.A.L.D. capture automatically and what requires human archival judgment?
These are fabrication and test assignments, not vetoes.
---
